## Why Asked
Tests understanding of system design patterns for traffic control and API reliability, crucial for distributed systems.

## Key Concepts
- Token Bucket: Allows bursts, refills at fixed rate
- Leaky Bucket: Smooths output, processes at constant rate
- Trade-offs: Burst capacity vs. consistent latency
- Use cases: API gateways, load balancing, DoS protection

## Code Example
```
// Token Bucket
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(capacity, refillRate) {
    this.tokens = capacity;
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.refillRate = refillRate;
    this.lastRefill = Date.now();
  }

  consume() {
    this.refill();
    if (this.tokens >= 1) {
      this.tokens--;
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }

  refill() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = (now - this.lastRefill) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + elapsed * this.refillRate);
    this.lastRefill = now;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement distributed rate limiting?
- What happens when the bucket is empty?
- How do you handle rate limiting in microservices?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests system design expertise, scalability knowledge, and production experience with high-traffic scenarios—critical skills for enterprise clients handling massive concurrent user loads.

## Expected Answer
Candidates should discuss: DNS-level load balancing with geographic distribution, L7 vs L4 load balancers, health checking strategies, circuit breaker patterns, auto-scaling policies, database sharding, multi-tier caching layers, comprehensive monitoring, and failover scenarios. Must address capacity planning and zero-downtime deployment strategies.

